\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces A chart of all the known irreducible particles of the standard model of particle physics. The three generations of quarks are shown in purple. The three generations of leptons are shown in green. The gauge bosons, which mediate the strong, weak, and electromagentic forces are shown in red. The Higgs boson is shown in yellow.}}{4}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The "Sombrero Potential" which spontaneously breaks the symmetry of an example U(1) group. The non-zero vacuum expecation value can be seen with a value of.}}{9}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Feynman diagrams of known Higgs production modes. a is known as gluon fusion, termed ggH. b is known as vector boson fusion (VBF). c is production of the Higgs in association with a vector boson (either a $W^{\pm }$ or $Z^0$ boson), called VH production. d is the production of the Higgs in association with a pair of top (ttH) or bottom (bbH) quarks. e and f both show Higgs production in association with a single top quark (tH). Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS10YearReview}}}{13}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces A pie chart of the relative proportions of the total $\sigma _H$ attributed to each production mode. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS10YearReview}.}}{14}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces Feynman diagrams of known Higgs decay modes. Heavy vector bosonic decays are shown in g, fermion-antifermion decays are shown in h, and photon/Z boson decays are shown in i and j. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS10YearReview}}}{15}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Theoretical branching fractions for a single Higgs. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS10YearReview}}}{17}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Feynman diagrams of known DiHiggs production modes. Figures k and l correspond to the two methods of ggH production, and m,n, and o correspond to the three methods of VBF production. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS10YearReview}.}}{19}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A simplified schematic of the overall layout of the LHC. The counter-rotating beams are shown in red (clockwise) and blue (counterclockwise). Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {LHC2008}. }}{23}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces A full view of the CMS Detector, with each major system labled. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS2024}.}}{26}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Longitudinal view of the pixel detector, where the beampipe is represented at the bottom of the image. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS2024}}}{28}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Full view of the tracker system. The inner pixel tracker is shown in green. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS2024}}}{29}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces A simplified schematic of the ECal. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {CMS2008}}}{30}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces A diagram of the L1 Trigger architecture, where the lefthand tree corresponds to the muon triggers and the righthand tree to the calorimeter triggers. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {L12020}.}}{36}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces A diagram of an L1 e/$\gamma $ cluster. The central seed tower is shown in red, and the clustered contiguous towers are shown in orange and yellow. The isolation region is shown in blue. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {L12020}.}}{38}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces A comparison of the percentage of the total rate allowed for each category of trigger. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {L12020}.}}{39}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces A distribution of the $\Delta \eta $ versus $\Delta \phi $ for the superclustering of simulated electrons. The z-axis represents the number of clusteres matched with the simulation around the seed. The red line represents the set of clusters selected by the mustache algorith. The seed cluster can be seen as the while region in the center of the plot (where both $\Delta \eta $ and $\Delta \phi $ are equal to 0). Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {OfflineReco2021}.}}{42}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces A pie chart showing the percentage of the full rate alloted for each physics object group for 2018 data. Note that this example of rate shares operates at a lower instantaneous luminosity and trigger rate than the Run3 data will have. Reproduced from \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {Run2HLT2024}.}}{44}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces On the left, the rates of the scouting dataset (top), prompt (middle, solid), and parking (middle, hatched) data streams are shown. Additionally, the instantaneous luminosity is shown (bottom). To the right, the menu timing is shown with CPU-only and CPU+GPU processing for the 2022 running year. Reproduced from (REF? LHCP PAPER)}}{46}{figure.caption.36}%
